{"kind":"report","text":"ready-for-review at exact clean head 46ccc0d6321055e898e4a27cb14533d92694a668, artifact be699f9722f641b38b219f1e834a910b8679511d.\n\nThis reports against the request differently from how it was written, and the difference is the point.\n\nThe request asked that admission turn on liveness rather than staleness, and that a stale-but-live clause admit with its staleness reported. That condition is not met and should not be. It rested on my belief that correct charter replacement makes a successor stale by construction, which is false: charter b86860f2 does not rest on its predecessor at all, and is stale only because a request I myself retired sits underneath it. Codex found this and I verified it directly against the log. There was never a case for widening.\n\nWhat the head does instead. Both boundaries fail closed. A retired charter or clause refuses because the admission was withdrawn; a stale one refuses because a basis moved and the thing that moved may be exactly the scope somebody took back. Admitting under it would convert a flare into continuing authority for an irreversible public write, and a printed warning re-authorizes nothing.\n\nTwo conditions of the request are met and were worth having. Each refusal names the statement and the reason, so an operator can repair a clause instead of being told there is none. And having no clause reads differently from refusing one, which is the ambiguity that sent me to file a false request against hugh in the first place.\n\nOne condition is met that the request did not ask for, because codex found the gap: a clause is now admitted only when its signed bases reach the charter the run acts under. Operator standing on the author says the actor may state clauses somewhere; it does not say this clause was granted under this doorstep. Without that check a clause from another charter, or from one since withdrawn, was honoured beneath whichever charter the run named. An unnamed charter anchors nothing, so a forgotten flag now admits nothing rather than everything.\n\nThe old test is replaced rather than deleted and now pins both refusals with distinct reasons, plus the charter-anchor rules. Everything is mutation-checked: dropping the anchor admits a foreign clause and one with no charter at all; restoring either widening fails the tests that pin it.\n\ngo vet and the full suite pass, and the worktree is clean at this head.\n\nThe parent request's dogfood is still open and now needs governance stated the right way round: a charter on a stable basis, ratified, the old one retired by a supersession naming it, and a fresh clause resting on the new charter. I have filed that to hugh corrected, and asked him to wait for this review before acting.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:be699f9722f641b38b219f1e834a910b8679511d","branch":"request/github-connector-render","head":"46ccc0d6321055e898e4a27cb14533d92694a668","status":"ready-for-review"}}